import json

extract_html = """<p><b><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ne" class="wiki-link" title="Neon (Ne)">Neon</a></b> (symbol <b>Ne</b>, atomic number 10) is a famous noble gas best known for its vivid, electric red-orange glow. Colorless, odorless, and completely tasteless under ordinary conditions, neon is a monatomic gas that is about two-thirds as dense as air. Positioned in Group 18 of the periodic table, neon is the fifth most abundant element in the universe, forged deep inside giant stars. Yet on Earth, it is extraordinarily rare, making up just 18.2 parts per million of our atmosphere. Known to theoretical chemists as the most inert and chemically aloof of all elements, neon refuses to form stable compounds under any known conditions. From illuminating iconic city skylines with glowing signage to driving ultra-precise microchip lasers and deep-space rock dating, neon is a tiny atom with a brilliant impact.</p>"""

history_html = """<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ne" class="wiki-link" title="Neon (Ne)">Neon</a> was discovered in London in June 1898 by British chemists Sir William Ramsay and Morris Travers. The duo isolated it by chilling samples of atmospheric air until it turned into a liquid, then carefully warming the liquid to capture different gases as they boiled off at distinct temperatures. After separating <a href="/school1/ptable/element/N" class="wiki-link" title="Nitrogen (N)">nitrogen</a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ar" class="wiki-link" title="Argon (Ar)">argon</a>, and the newly found <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Kr" class="wiki-link" title="Krypton (Kr)">krypton</a>, they electrified the remaining trapped gas inside a vacuum tube. Immediately, the gas burst into an unforgettably intense, brilliant crimson-red light. Travers later fondly recalled, <i>"the blaze of crimson light from the tube told its own story and was a sight to dwell upon and never forget."</i> Ramsay's young son suggested naming the element "neon," derived from <i>neos</i>, the Greek word for "new" (analogous to the Latin <i>novum</i>). During their search, the team also briefly reported a gas named <i>metargon</i>, which was later revealed to be argon mixed with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">carbon</a> monoxide, before going on to discover <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Xe" class="wiki-link" title="Xenon (Xe)">xenon</a> in September 1898.</p>
<p>Co-discoverer Morris Travers shares a momentous historical link with India. In 1906, known in scientific circles as "Rare Gas Travers," he moved to India to serve as the founding Director of the prestigious Indian Institute of Science (IISc) in Bangalore. Working alongside the vision of industrialist Jamsetji Tata and the Maharaja of Mysore, Travers spent nearly a decade building IISc's original campus, setting up its premier chemistry laboratories, and establishing the groundwork for modern scientific and cryogenic research in India.</p>
<p>Because neon is so sparse in the atmosphere, it could not be immediately used for lighting like earlier nitrogen-filled Moore tubes. However, in 1902, French engineer Georges Claude began producing industrial quantities of neon as a byproduct of his air-liquefaction company, Air Liquide. Claude patented the sealed neon light tube in December 1910. While his attempt to sell neon for home indoor lighting failed because people found the intense red color overwhelming, his business partner realized its potential for eye-catching outdoor advertising in 1912. Neon signs debuted in the United States in 1923 when a Packard car dealership in Los Angeles bought two glowing signs. The mesmerizing red glow captured the public imagination, transforming 20th-century cityscapes into dazzling displays of "electro-graphic architecture."</p>
<p>Neon also played a monumental role in unveiling the hidden structure of atoms. In 1913, legendary physicist J. J. Thomson funneled ionized neon beams through electric and magnetic fields toward a photographic plate. Instead of a single curve, he observed two distinct parabolic patches of light. Thomson realized that neon gas contained atoms of two different masses—specifically neon-20 and neon-22. This historic experiment provided the very first evidence that stable chemical elements exist as different isotopes, and Thomson's setup served as the direct forerunner to the modern mass spectrometer.</p>"""

characteristics_html = """<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ne" class="wiki-link" title="Neon (Ne)">Neon</a> is the second-lightest noble gas, positioned between <a href="/school1/ptable/element/He" class="wiki-link" title="Helium (He)">helium</a> and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ar" class="wiki-link" title="Argon (Ar)">argon</a>. In its natural state, it is completely colorless, odorless, and non-toxic. However, when placed inside an evacuated tube and excited by an electric voltage, neon plasma produces the most intense light discharge of all noble gases. To the human eye, this light glows a vivid red-orange due to strong emission lines in that region, though spectroscopic analysis reveals a hidden green emission line buried within its spectrum.</p>
<p>From a chemical standpoint, neon holds the title of the least reactive element in the entire periodic table. While helium has a higher ionization energy, quantum mechanical calculations show that helium can form exotic species under extreme pressures—such as the helium hydride ion (HeH⁺), solid Na₂He, and theoretical helium hydride fluoride (HHeF). By contrast, equivalent neon molecules like HNeF are completely unbound and unstable. While chemists have theoretically proposed a complex neon anion [B₁₂(CN)₁₁Ne]⁻, no stable neutral or covalent neon compound has ever been synthesized or observed in a laboratory under any temperature or pressure. Neon stands alone as the only noble gas with zero confirmed chemical compounds.</p>
<p>In cryogenic applications, liquid neon is an extraordinary refrigerant. On a volume basis, liquid neon has over 40 times the cooling capacity of liquid helium and three times that of liquid <a href="/school1/ptable/element/H" class="wiki-link" title="Hydrogen (H)">hydrogen</a>. Because it boils at 27.104 K (−246.046 °C) and melts at 24.56 K (−248.59 °C), liquid neon provides intense cooling power in a compact range where liquid helium would be far more expensive and prone to rapid evaporation.</p>"""

isotopes_html = """<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ne" class="wiki-link" title="Neon (Ne)">Neon</a> possesses three stable natural isotopes: <sup>20</sup>Ne (90.48%), <sup>21</sup>Ne (0.27%), and <sup>22</sup>Ne (9.25%). The vast majority of <sup>20</sup>Ne is primordial material forged in ancient stars. By contrast, <sup>21</sup>Ne and <sup>22</sup>Ne are partly primordial and partly "nucleogenic," created inside rocks when alpha particles and neutrons from decaying <a href="/school1/ptable/element/U" class="wiki-link" title="Uranium (U)">uranium</a> collide with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mg" class="wiki-link" title="Magnesium (Mg)">magnesium</a> atoms (<sup>24</sup>Mg and <sup>25</sup>Mg). Because of this reaction, uranium-rich rocks like granites exhibit lower <sup>20</sup>Ne/<sup>22</sup>Ne ratios and elevated <sup>21</sup>Ne/<sup>22</sup>Ne signatures.</p>
<p>High-energy cosmic rays hitting exposed surface rocks also create "cosmogenic" <sup>21</sup>Ne through spallation reactions on <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Mg" class="wiki-link" title="Magnesium (Mg)">magnesium</a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Na" class="wiki-link" title="Sodium (Na)">sodium</a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Si" class="wiki-link" title="Silicon (Si)">silicon</a>, and aluminum atoms. In India, planetary scientists at the Physical Research Laboratory (PRL) in Ahmedabad utilize high-precision Noble Gas Mass Spectrometry to measure cosmogenic <sup>21</sup>Ne trapped inside meteorites that fall across the Indian subcontinent (such as the Kamargaon and Rantila meteorites). By measuring these neon isotopes, PRL researchers accurately calculate the cosmic ray exposure (CRE) age of the meteorites, revealing how many millions of years they drifted through open space before crashing to Earth.</p>
<p>Isotopic signatures also offer clues about the solar system's origins. Solar wind neon is exceptionally rich in <sup>20</sup>Ne compared to Earth sources. Because volcanic gases and deep mantle diamonds on Earth are also enriched in <sup>20</sup>Ne, geologists suspect that trapped primordial solar gas was locked inside Earth's deep interior during its formation. Except for extremely rare cluster decays of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Th" class="wiki-link" title="Thorium (Th)">thorium</a>-228 that produce <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a>-20 (which decays into <sup>20</sup>Ne), <sup>20</sup>Ne is not produced by radioactive decay, making its distribution across Earth a subject of lively scientific study.</p>"""

occurrence_html = """<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ne" class="wiki-link" title="Neon (Ne)">Neon</a> is created in massive stars (greater than 8 solar masses) during the carbon-burning phase of stellar evolution. When core temperatures exceed 500 million kelvins (500 megakelvins), two <a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">carbon</a> nuclei fuse together to produce <sup>20</sup>Ne. Across the cosmos, neon is the fifth most abundant chemical element by mass—behind <a href="/school1/ptable/element/H" class="wiki-link" title="Hydrogen (H)">hydrogen</a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/He" class="wiki-link" title="Helium (He)">helium</a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a>, and carbon—making up about 1 part in 750 of all universal mass (and 1 part in 600 in the Sun).</p>
<p>Despite its cosmic abundance, neon is surprisingly scarce on Earth, comprising only 18.2 parts per million (1 in 55,000) of Earth's atmosphere by volume, or 1 part in 79,000 by mass. Because neon is a light, single-atom gas with high vapor pressure at low temperatures, most of the primordial neon escaped into space while the warm, solid Earth was forming. However, because a single neon atom is lighter than diatomic <a href="/school1/ptable/element/N" class="wiki-link" title="Nitrogen (N)">nitrogen</a> (N₂) and oxygen (O₂) molecules, a balloon filled with neon will float in air, though more slowly than a helium balloon.</p>
<p>Beyond Earth, NASA's LADEE spacecraft confirmed in August 2015 that neon exists in the thin exosphere of the Moon. Meanwhile, the Galileo probe revealed that Jupiter's upper atmosphere is strangely depleted of neon (only 1 part in 6,000 by mass, about one-tenth of solar levels). Planetary scientists hypothesize that neon either dissolved into deep metallic liquid hydrogen drops inside Jupiter or that the icy planetesimals that built Jupiter formed in regions too warm to trap neon.</p>
<p>On Earth, neon is harvested industrially through the cryogenic fractional distillation of liquid air. Large-scale air separation plants across India—such as those operated by INOX Air Products and Linde India—chill atmospheric air down to liquid temperatures, separating high-purity neon alongside industrial oxygen, nitrogen, and argon.</p>"""

applications_html = """<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ne" class="wiki-link" title="Neon (Ne)">Neon</a> is famous for its glowing orange-red advertising signs, high-voltage test indicators, surge arresters, and plasma display panels. However, its most critical modern role is in advanced laser technology. Helium-Neon (He-Ne) gas lasers emit a highly stable red beam at 632.8 nanometers. Pioneered in India by institutions like Bhabha Atomic Research Centre (BARC) and the Raja Ramanna Centre for Advanced Technology (RRCAT) in Indore, He-Ne lasers are widely used for precision optical alignment, metrology, biological research, and laboratory education.</p>
<p>In global high-tech manufacturing, neon plays an indispensable behind-the-scenes role in microchip production. Semiconductor fabrication relies heavily on excimer lasers (such as Argon-Fluoride and Krypton-Fluoride lasers), which use high-purity neon as a buffer gas. These lasers generate deep ultraviolet light to etch microscopic circuitry onto silicon wafers. As India expands its domestic semiconductor ecosystem under the India Semiconductor Mission, securing high-purity neon gas from cryogenic air separation units has become a vital strategic focus.</p>
<p>In cryogenic engineering, liquid neon offers extraordinary compact cooling power for specialized scientific sensors, superconducting equipment, and aerospace instruments. In deep-sea diving, neon-oxygen gas mixtures are sometimes used in experimental breathing blends to reduce the narcotic effects caused by nitrogen under high pressure. Combining vivid aesthetic beauty with cutting-edge physics, neon remains a silent titan of modern technology.</p>"""

